WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.825
VOICE OVER: If you’ve turned on a light&nbsp;
outside at night, you’ve probably seen this.

00:00:05.825 --> 00:00:10.080
SAMUEL FABIAN: As soon as humans started&nbsp;
lighting fires or creating light at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.680
they noticed that seemingly suicidally&nbsp;
insects would dive into them or kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.040
emerge out the night and cluster around&nbsp;
these light sources, and we wondered why.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:22.520
VO: Previous explanations have included&nbsp;
insects using the moon to navigate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.080
or simply being drawn to the heat&nbsp;
given off by incandescent bulbs.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:31.640
Now though advances in camera technology are&nbsp;
allowing researchers to study the flight of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.680
these insects in more detail than ever before,&nbsp;
and this group have made a key discovery.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.400
Flying insects seem to be twisting to&nbsp;
keep their back to the light. Rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.840
than being attracted towards it, they find&nbsp;
themselves stuck in a loop flying around it.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:59.920
Have these experiments finally answered the&nbsp;
question - what draws the moth to the flame?

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:05.720
VO: Moths' attraction to light is well known&nbsp;
enough to have inspired a commonly used phrase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.040
but it’s not just moths that are&nbsp;
attracted to artificial lights.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.280
SAM: If you're stood by a light at night,&nbsp;
and you see something batting around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.640
it's probably not just moths, there are&nbsp;
flies, there are wasps, there's any number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.040
of different insects. Basically any insect&nbsp;
that can fly, more or less, is going to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.520
up at lights at night. It just so happens that&nbsp;
a whole bunch of insects rarely fly at night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.520
and so an example of that would be the dragonfly,&nbsp;
we we we tend not to think about dragonflies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.760
coming lights at night, but actually it&nbsp;
turns out if you get a dragonfly to fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.640
at night, it ends up circling around&nbsp;
the light just like your moth does.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.500
VO: And even though this behaviour is very&nbsp;
common, explaining it has proven tricky.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.880
SAM: The real problem is that insects move&nbsp;
really fast for their body size. So when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.760
film it turns out as a little blur even under&nbsp;
quite bright light and then you take that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.240
night where you have to use infrared lights in&nbsp;
order to illuminate the insect but not have it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.760
see the light, it's only been recently that we've&nbsp;
had sensitive enough cameras to allow us to film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.580
insects at night, at speeds that then allow us to&nbsp;
see what the insect is actually doing in the air.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:13.720
VO: The team attach reflective markers to the&nbsp;
insects’ bodies. Then, eight motion capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:20.080
cameras allow them to track its exact position&nbsp;
and orientation at 200 frames per second.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.280
SAM: The camera can detect these&nbsp;
little markers on the backs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.080
the insect and we can get a really&nbsp;
accurate read on exactly where it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.360
in space and actually we can tell&nbsp;
the orientation in space as well.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.000
VO: As well as this lab data, the&nbsp;
team made observations in the wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.400
under more natural conditions.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.160
SAM: Either in our back gardens filming things&nbsp;
or traveling down to Costa Rica in order to film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.720
large numbers of different kinds of night&nbsp;
flying insect around light sources. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:49.360
where we're using high frame rate cameras under&nbsp;
kind of constant infrared illumination to record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.760
insects traveling around light sources&nbsp;
and that come to light sources at night.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.560
VO: Together these data revealed exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.480
was happening as the insects&nbsp;
approached a light source.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.960
SAM: What we kept finding is that&nbsp;
insects such as dragonflies, moths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:09.080
butterflies and other night flying insects as&nbsp;
well, were tilting their backs - uh which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:14.480
call the dorsal axis - their dorsum towards&nbsp;
the light. And this matches onto a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.880
we've known about for quite some time in fact,&nbsp;
it's been written about in the literature for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.680
probably about the last 100 years, and&nbsp;
that's called a dorsal light response.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.120
VO: This so called dorsal light response has&nbsp;
been described for some time in insects and fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.540
and is believed to be used to help them&nbsp;
orient themselves when moving in 3D space.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:36.040
SAM: It's quite strange for us to think&nbsp;
about this because as animals that spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.400
most of our time on the ground it's quite&nbsp;
obvious which way gravity is, but actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:44.560
if you're flying you're pulling all kinds of&nbsp;
g-forces and those g-forces or accelerations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:50.280
as you're moving around can kind of mask exactly&nbsp;
where true gravity is especially if there's some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.800
sort of big aerial turbulence that hits you, you&nbsp;
want to very quickly work out which way is up.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.840
VO: And before humans and&nbsp;
their lightbulbs came along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.440
looking for the relative brightness of the&nbsp;
sky compared to the earth was a good way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.000
finding up. So insects tilt their backs&nbsp;
towards the brightest area they can see.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.200
SAM: That means that all of their&nbsp;
flight forces they're producing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.120
the lift is they're not pointing in the&nbsp;
right direction for them to continue flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:18.080
they're going to start curving. And so that's why&nbsp;
we see them circling, and not really spiralling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.060
in, cruising around and round and round and&nbsp;
seeming completely stuck and unable to leave.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:26.700
VO: And this isn’t the only problem this&nbsp;
simple reflex causes insects around lights.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.000
SAM: If we give them light from beneath we&nbsp;
actually see that insects totally invert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.920
themselves and flip upside down, and that&nbsp;
obviously is not a good way to get about at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.080
night because they then crash straight on into&nbsp;
the ground, and that's probably why if you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.320
light shining on a kind of patch of floor you&nbsp;
tend to get insects sort of plummeting out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.160
the darkness straight onto that floor. They flip&nbsp;
themselves upside down and crashed, and we also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.440
get stalling which is where insects that are&nbsp;
flying away from the light begin to pitch up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.440
pitch up, and they're then at not producing&nbsp;
enough flight force to maintain the climb that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.460
they're attempting to do and that causes them&nbsp;
to slow down and tip onto new flight courses.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:05.120
VO: Other explanations for this behaviour&nbsp;
that have been put forward over the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.520
don’t seem to match the new&nbsp;
observations.. The insects in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.040
this study weren’t attracted to heat -&nbsp;
nor were they keeping the lights at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:17.080
same angle relative to their body as they&nbsp;
would if trying to navigate by the moon.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.120
So is this case closed for the&nbsp;
mystery of the mesmerizing effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.140
of artificial light on flying insects? Not quite…

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:31.880
SAM: There are several species of night flying&nbsp;
insects that we don't find at light traps and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:35.680
I really want to get hold of those because I want&nbsp;
to see whether or not they still have this dorsal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.080
light response. Because if they're not using&nbsp;
light particularly to work out which way is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.280
up and we know that they're flying near lights&nbsp;
but don't ever seem to get entrapped that's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.280
very strong link to draw that it is really this&nbsp;
behavioral phenomenon that leads to all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.320
insects coming to light at night, because we&nbsp;
cannot yet say absolutely this is the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.940
influence of the light on the insect while it's&nbsp;
flying around there may be additional factors.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.920
VO: This study only looked at insect&nbsp;
behaviour within a few metres of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.320
light source, but what happens&nbsp;
when insects are further away.

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.800
SAM: We'd like to ecologically contextualize&nbsp;
this behavior of coming to light and determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.760
exactly what the kind of capture radius around&nbsp;
a light source would be at night and how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:21.760
affects insects that are doing different tasks&nbsp;
around an ecosystem. So whether that be foraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.400
over flowers or whether that be migrating&nbsp;
at high altitude directly over overhead.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.320
VO: And understanding this mechanism doesn’t&nbsp;
just answer an age-old question - it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.260
lead to new ways to preserve and&nbsp;
protect these flying insects.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.200
SAM: How could we change lighting environments&nbsp;
to not trap insects because we're facing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.640
massive decline in insects around the world&nbsp;
and artificial light at night is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.280
factors that could potentially be leading to&nbsp;
this decline. So only through understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.120
the actual mechanism that causes insects&nbsp;
to get trapped around light can we begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:59.400
to understand how we could prevent them&nbsp;
getting trapped around light sources.

